# Commit messages

Every commit subject must be **descriptive enough that a stranger can tell what changed without opening the diff**.

## Required shape

- One line: `Area/Day: concrete outcome - YES`
- **Max 50 characters** for the entire subject (including the ` - YES` suffix)
- Always end with ` - YES` (see `03-session-roadmap.mdc`)
- Prefer why / outcome over file lists; name the Day when the work is a roadmap Day
- Optional body (2–4 lines) when the subject cannot capture a multi-crate change
- Do **not** add `Co-authored-by: Cursor` (or any Cursor agent trailer)

## Reject (rewrite before committing)

Subjects that are only a label, number, or buzzword — even if they fit in 50 chars:

- `fix ci - YES`, `CI fix - YES`, `arch - YES`, `pages - YES`
- `Day 40 - YES`, `day 32 - YES`, `Day 38 - YES` (Day number alone is not enough)
- `update - YES`, `wip - YES`, `misc - YES`, `docs - YES`

Also reject any subject longer than 50 characters; shorten outcome wording first, put detail in the body.

## Prefer (all ≤50 chars)

- `fix(ci): pin LiteSVM 0.10 for CI - YES`
- `Day 40: Month 2 audit + harness - YES`
- `docs: slim README, pin toolchain - YES`
- `security: ignore RUSTSEC-2026-0344 - YES`

If the draft subject could fit many unrelated diffs, rewrite it from the staged change summary before `git commit`.
